📦 Data Engineering Q&A Pack (SQL Server → GCP → BigQuery)

Q1: How do you handle bad records during batch processing?
- Run DQ checks (nulls, duplicates, schema validation).
- Quarantine bad records into error bucket for audit.
- Allow clean data to flow downstream so batch completes.
- Reprocess bad records later once corrected.
Follow-up: Why not stop the batch?
→ SLA must be met. Clean data continues, bad data is isolated and reprocessed later.

Q2: If the issue is upstream (source), how do you reprocess?
- Identify root cause with source team.
- Quarantine bad records.
- Request corrected data or apply cleansing logic.
- Reload only affected partitions in BigQuery.
- Validate again before merging into silver/gold.
Follow-up: How do you avoid reprocessing the whole dataset?
→ Use partition-level reprocessing (overwrite/merge only impacted date).

Q3: Batch already completed, corrected data arrives later. What do you do?
- Stage corrected data in GCS.
- Merge into BigQuery using MERGE or overwrite partition.
- Check orchestration logs/metadata tables to see if downstream jobs ran.
- If yes, re-trigger downstream DAGs for that partition (Airflow backfill).
- Validate again to ensure consistency.

Q4: How do you implement CDC or incremental loads from SQL Server to BigQuery?
- Initial full load → baseline snapshot.
- Then incremental loads daily/hourly using:
   • SQL Server CDC tables
   • Last modified timestamp
   • GCP Datastream (log-based CDC)
- Stage incremental data in GCS.
- Use Dataflow/PySpark for DQ checks.
- Merge into BigQuery partitions with MERGE.
Follow-up: What if source doesn’t support CDC or timestamps?
→ Use hash-based comparison between snapshots, reload recent partitions if append-only, or log-based capture via Datastream/Debezium. Maintain watermark metadata in BigQuery.

Q5: How do you stage only changed data in GCS if it doesn’t support CDC?
- CDC/incremental filtering happens at extraction stage (SQL queries or Datastream).
- Only changed rows are extracted and written into GCS.
- GCS stores partitioned files (by date/time).
- Dataflow merges into BigQuery.

Q6: What if source has no CDC and no timestamp column?
- Full load initially → baseline snapshot.
- Then:
   • Hash-based comparison (row-level checksums).
   • Partition reload strategy (reload last N days).
   • Log-based capture via GCP Datastream.
- Store incremental extracts in GCS.
- Merge into BigQuery with partition overwrite/merge.

